    <h2>How Battery Storage Works</h2>
    <p>Without a battery, any solar power you don't use immediately gets exported to the grid — usually for a much lower rate than you'd pay to buy it back later. A battery stores that surplus power instead, so you can use it after the sun goes down, cutting how much you rely on grid electricity.</p>

    <h2>Typical Battery Costs</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Small battery (2.5–5kWh):</strong> £2,500 – £4,000</li>
      <li><strong>Mid-size battery (5–10kWh):</strong> £4,000 – £7,000</li>
      <li><strong>Large battery (10kWh+):</strong> £7,000 – £10,000+</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Most UK homes with a typical 3-4 bedroom property and a standard solar system are well suited to a battery in the 5-10kWh range.</p>

    <h2>How Much Can You Actually Save?</h2>
    <p>Savings depend heavily on your household's energy usage pattern. If you're home and using electricity mostly during the day, a battery adds less value since you're already using your solar power directly. If you're out during the day and use most of your electricity in the evening, a battery can significantly increase how much of your own solar generation you actually use — often pushing self-consumption from around 40% up to 70-80%.</p>

    <h2>Payback Period</h2>
    <p>Battery payback periods are typically longer than solar panels alone — usually <strong>8 to 12 years</strong>, depending on your usage pattern and electricity prices. Some households also use batteries to take advantage of cheaper overnight electricity tariffs, charging the battery from the grid at low rates and using that power during expensive peak hours, which can shorten payback further.</p>

    <h2>Is It Worth It?</h2>
    <p>A battery isn't essential for solar to be worthwhile — many households get excellent returns from panels alone. But if you're rarely home during daylight hours, have a large system that regularly generates more than you use, or want backup power during outages, a battery can make a strong case for itself.</p>

    <h2>The Bottom Line</h2>
    <p>Battery storage works best when it's matched to your actual usage pattern, not added as a default. Getting a personalised assessment based on your energy habits and existing (or planned) solar system is the best way to know if the payback makes sense for your home.</p>

